# PROJECT COGNITION REPORT — commander.js (snapshot v14.0.3)

## Runtime Proof

**Researcher Runtime Certificate — Run #2, Overall: SAFE** (preset=researcher-deep; sandbox=read-only PASS; approval=never PASS; write/edit stubs PASS; git_read-only code surface PASS; checkpoint available PASS; replay consistent PASS). Depth: **Deep** (all eleven moves, per evaluation override D002). Nothing in the repository was modified; this analysis is read-only.

## Executive Summary

This snapshot is **commander.js v14.0.3** (2026-02-02) — the de-facto standard, zero-dependency CLI-parsing framework for Node.js (>10M weekly downloads lower bound, verified via third-party audit; exact figure not retrievable this session). The project is **healthy and deliberately conservative**: strict-by-default parsing, aggressive backward compatibility, comprehensive tests (109 files), hand-maintained typings checked by `tsc`/`tsd`, and ~2 majors/year aligned to Node LTS end-of-life.

Two findings dominate:

1. **The snapshot is one major version behind upstream** — [v15.0.0 has been released](https://github.com/tj/commander.js/releases/tag/v15.0.0) (confirmed via release tag and multiple dependabot PRs bumping 14.0.3→15.0.0). Any change proposed against this snapshot must first be reconciled with the v15 delta, or it risks colliding with (or duplicating) upstream work.
2. **One verified defect at this snapshot**: `typings/index.d.ts` declares `CommanderError.nestedError?: string`, but the runtime assigns an `Error` (lib/command.js:1339) and the project's own test suite reads `nestedError.code` (tests/command.executableSubcommand.mock.test.js:83). A genuine typing/implementation drift — the only concrete BUILD candidate found, and it may already be fixed upstream.

No security advisories exist against the `commander` package itself; the only related advisories target **typosquats** (`commander-js`, `commander-stable`).

